Ingrown Toenail Wedge Resection
General Practice Other
Painful erythematous nail fold, Penetration of nail plate, Hypertrophic granulation tissue, Digital nerve block

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: GPO-020
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe exacerbation of a chronic ingrown toenail, rendering the patient entirely unable to wear footwear or mobilize for work.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a patient with an acutely infected, deeply embedded ingrown toenail experiencing severe throbbing pain and active purulent discharge, resulting in an absolute inability to tolerate any footwear or walk. Urgent face-to-face assessment was strictly required to administer a digital nerve block for immediate pain relief, perform a wedge resection to remove the offending nail spicule, and establish drainage of the localized abscess. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period would have left the patient completely incapacitated and in severe, unmanageable pain overnight.

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: "My big toe is throbbing so badly I can't even let the bedsheets touch it, and I absolutely cannot put a work boot on."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with severe throbbing pain localized to the lateral nail fold of the right hallux. Over the past three weeks, there has been increasing erythema, swelling, and induration of the affected periungual tissue. The patient reports a visible penetration of the nail plate deep into the adjacent soft tissue. Over the last 48 hours, the patient noted the onset of purulent discharge and localized abscess formation, accompanied by hypertrophic granulation tissue overlapping the nail plate. The pain is severely exacerbated by wearing closed shoes or direct pressure, entirely preventing occupational duties.
Functional Status: Antalgic gait; completely unable to wear closed footwear.
Social/Safety Concerns: Immediate threat to occupational status as the patient cannot mobilize.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 135/85 | HR: 90 | Temp: 37.1 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 80 Kg
General: Uncomfortable, wearing an open sandal on the affected foot, guarding the toe.
Physical Exam: Integumentary: Examination of the right hallux reveals a severely painful, erythematous lateral nail fold. There is active penetration of the nail plate into the soft tissue, accompanied by a 5mm area of friable, hypertrophic granulation tissue that bleeds easily on contact. A small amount of purulent discharge is expressible. The procedure requires the administration of a digital nerve block utilizing plain lidocaine, followed by the excision of the offending longitudinal sliver of the nail plate. Expected investigation results include direct clinical visualization of the nail spicule impinging on the lateral nail fold, strictly without the necessity for radiographic imaging assuming an absence of underlying osteomyelitis.
Environmental Observations: Clean environment suitable for a sterile podiatric procedure.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Ingrown Toenail (Onychocryptosis with Secondary Paronychia) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Subungual Melanoma - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The acute, painful, inflammatory nature of the presentation with visible mechanical penetration by the nail, rather than a painless, progressive pigmented streak beneath an intact nail.

Herpetic Whitlow - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of clustered, painful vesicles containing clear fluid; presentation is purely driven by mechanical impingement and secondary bacterial infection.

Gouty Arthritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The inflammation is strictly isolated to the lateral nail fold and soft tissue, sparing the metatarsophalangeal joint which has full, painless range of motion.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The patient suffered from an acutely infected and mechanically embedded ingrown toenail, causing debilitating localized pain and total functional impairment regarding footwear. Telephone triage correctly identified the mechanical nature of the problem, which is completely unresponsive to systemic antibiotics without source control. Urgent face-to-face assessment was necessary to perform an immediate digital ring block, providing total anesthesia, followed by a surgical wedge resection to physically extract the penetrating nail spicule. Deferring this intervention until the next in-hours period would have guaranteed another night of agonizing pain, continued infection, and total inability to mobilize the following day.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Administered a digital nerve block to the right hallux using 1% Lidocaine (plain). Once fully anesthetized, performed a partial nail avulsion (wedge resection) to extract the deep offending spicule. Cauterized the hypergranulation tissue with silver nitrate. Applied a chemical matrixectomy utilizing phenol to the lateral horn to prevent recurrence. Applied a bulky sterile dressing. Prescribed oral Cephalexin 500mg QID for 5 days.
Positive Behavioral Change: To promote healing and prevent future mechanical trauma to the nail bed, strictly commit to cutting your toenails straight across rather than curving the edges. Place your nail clippers in a small box alongside a visual reminder note saying "STRAIGHT ONLY." By physically separating the tool and forcing a visual check before every use, you break the unconscious habit of aggressively rounding the corners, permanently reducing the risk of recurrence.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP or podiatrist review in 3 days for a wound check and redressing.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Call 000 or present to the nearest Emergency Department if you develop rapidly spreading redness up your foot and ankle, if you develop a systemic fever, or if the toe turns completely white or blue and does not regain normal color.

